"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets the general mechanisms right but has substantive omissions in both the US example and the UCITS rules.\\n\\nWhat it covers well:\\n- It separates borrower default, collateral shortfall and cash-reinvestment loss as distinct risks.\\n- It gives reasonable controls for each.\\n- It keeps the US and UCITS sections separate.\\n- It says collateral reduces but does not eliminate losses.\\n- On UCITS, it correctly covers the ability to recall at any time, liquid and diversified collateral, haircuts, and restricted cash reinvestment.\\n\\nOmissions in the US section:\\n- It admits it lacks the document and gives only generic practice (agent lending, indemnification, contractual recalls).\\n- It does not state that borrower failure can delay recovery or leave collateral short of replacement cost.\\n- It does not state that iShares keeps collateral at least equal to the current market value of securities lent.\\n\\nOmissions in the UCITS section:\\n- It does not mention daily valuation or credit quality as collateral requirements.\\n- It does not state that non-cash collateral cannot be reinvested.\\n\\nNo material errors, but the requested US example is largely missing.\"}","ttft_ms":3344,"type":"result","duration_ms":6591,"uuid":"1fd33741-0307-4d60-93f6-f88ba6945473","ttft_stream_ms":579,"time_to_request_ms":70,"first_content_frame_ms":580,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
